sections.content:
A facade coordinating real subsystems is untestable if it news up its dependencies internally. Consider this anti-pattern:

```js
class OrderFacade {
  constructor() {
    this._payment = new StripeGateway(); // hardwired — cannot substitute
    this._email   = new SendGridClient(); // hardwired — network call in tests
  }
}
```

To test `placeOrder`, you must either hit the real Stripe API (slow, costly, stateful) or monkey-patch the module (fragile). Neither is acceptable for a unit test.

The solution is **constructor injection**: accept subsystem instances as parameters with sensible defaults.

```js
class OrderFacade {
  constructor({ payment = new StripeGateway(), email = new SendGridClient() } = {}) {
    this._payment = payment;
    this._email   = email;
  }
}
```

Now a test can inject fakes: `new OrderFacade({ payment: fakePayment, email: fakeEmail })`. The facade's coordination logic — the sequencing, the error compensation, the conditional email — is tested without any real network calls.

This pattern has a name in the testing literature: **Humble Object**. The facade is a thin coordination shell (humble — easily testable) that delegates to real subsystems (which are tested independently). The complexity is in the subsystems; the sequence is in the facade; the test verifies the sequence without running the complexity.

In TypeScript, you would define interfaces for each subsystem and inject them:

```ts
interface IPaymentGateway {
  charge(userId: string, amount: number): Promise<Receipt>;
  refund(receiptId: string): Promise<void>;
}

class OrderFacade {
  constructor(
    private readonly payment: IPaymentGateway,
    private readonly email: IEmailService,
    private readonly inventory: IInventoryService,
  ) {}
}
```

NestJS's dependency injection container does exactly this: it resolves the concrete implementations at runtime while the facade's constructor declares only interface contracts.

